What is SUE?
------------

Mostly TCL/TK procedures
	procedures are "hidden" and embedded into C executable

	instances.tcl write.tcl icon_write.tcl icon.tcl
   ~    canvas.tcl schem_canvas.tcl make_schematic.tcl
   3    draw_wire.tcl arc.tcl lines.tcl text.tcl	- create items
   0    sticky_move.tcl duplication.tcl edit_marker.tcl	- change
   K    windows.tcl connect.tcl selection.tcl
        netlist.tcl net_spice.tcl net_verilog.tcl net_flat.tcl	- netlisting
   l    verilog_probe.tcl spice_probe.tcl sim_probe.tcl	- probing
   i    dpc.tcl	dpc_utils.tcl timing.tcl		- dpc
   n    prop_edit.tcl zoom.tcl help.tcl api.tcl print.tcl	- misc.
   e    share_utils.tcl utilities.tcl prop_menu.tcl	- utils
   s    call_by_keyword.tcl dialog.tcl FSBox.tcl	- more utils

Schematics are TK Canvases:
	Special Micro Magic version
		rotated text (one rotation only)
		argument to closest to exclude current
		coordsall functions for undo
		new tag implementation for speed

Called from a shell script called "sue"
	sets up extended fonts
	provides command line help
	calls C executable

sueAppInit.c
	license manager stuff
		SUE version
		Gets DPC license at dpc netlist

	sets up special MMI procs for protection

sueInitA.tcl
	first TCL file executed
	sets up global variables
	reads .suerc files

sueInitB.tcl
	executed after all TCL programs loaded
	sets up windows
	executes command line switches

Global Variables
	almost all global variables are upper case
	naming: foo_bar, NOT fooBar
	no protection from user stomping on


TK Canvases
------------

TK only updates the screen when idle or code issues an "update"
	packing and unpacking windows is an exception

Every different cell has its own canvas, one for schematic and one for icon
	global cur_c --> name of current canvas (e.g. .win1.c_test)
	global cur_s --> name of current cell (e.g. test or ICON_test)
	global scale --> scale of current canvas

Every element in a canvas has a unique ID associated with it

Items are grouped together with tags
	TK permits operations on an id or on every id with a given tag
	all elements of an icon have the tags inst<id> where <id>
	  is the id of the icon origin

Example:

            hello
                           |\
	        foo|=>-----|A)O-
                           |/

    sue> info_canvas
    88 line origin icon icon_INVA inst88 selected
    89 rectangle icon inst88 term {name term input in} selected
    90 rectangle icon inst88 term {name term output out} selected
    91 line icon inst88 selected
    92 arc icon inst88 arc selected
    93 text icon inst88 size_large scaletext selected
    95 line wire
    97 line open
    98 line origin icon icon_input inst98
    99 line icon inst98
    100 rectangle icon inst98 term {name io input foo}
    101 text icon inst98 size_standard scaletext
    102 text draw_item size_standard scaletext

    sue> info_canvas origin
    88 line origin icon icon_INVA inst88 selected
    98 line origin icon icon_input inst98

    [id] [type] [tag] [tag] ...

    Note: the inverter is selected.

Canvases are infinite in extent, displaying only a window of full contents

Canvases cannot be scaled (!).
	Must scale contents of canvases instead
	Scale text by resizing all text
	canvases are saved at scale=10
	scale=1 means different wires can be on adjacent pixels on screen


Schematic and Icon Procedures
------------------------------

All schematics and icons have TCL procedures associated with them
	SCHEMATIC_<cell>, ICON_<cell>
	Note: cur_s is <cell> for schematic, ICON_<cell> for icon

Schematic and icon procedures fill canvases
	"SCHEMATIC_<cell>" makes a schematic
	"make <cell>" makes an instance of the icon
	"icon_make <cell>" makes an icon canvas (internal procedure)

Build procedures from canvases and data structures (see below)
	write_schematic --> SCHEMATIC_<cell>
	write_icon --> ICON_<cell>
	scale set to 10 and numbers rounded to integer

Can't build procedures from generated icons since they are programs



SUE's Compiler
--------------

TCL is too slow to simply execute ICON procedures every time you
move/copy/place/change an icon.

ICON procedures are compiled into much simpler code for speed.
	ICON_<cell> --> _MAKE_<cell> and _MAKE90_<cell>

Two procedures, one rotated 90 degrees, along with scaling makes 8 orientations
	scale x=1, y=-1 --> mirror around y axis
	scale x=-1, y=1 --> mirror around x axis

Example:

    proc ICON_input {args} {
      icon_setup $args {{origin {0 0}} {orient R0} {name {}}}
      icon_line 0 0 -10 0 -15 -5 -25 -5 -25 5 -15 05 -10 0
      icon_term -type input -origin {0 0} -name {$name} -priority input
      icon_property -origin {-40 50} -type user -name name
      icon_property -origin {-35 0} -label {$name} -anchor e
      icon_property -origin {-40 70} -type fixed -name primitive -text 1
    }

    proc _MAKE_input {orient name} {
      global cur_c cur_s scale FONT XFORM ICON_ERROR env
      set fscale [expr int(ceil($scale))]
      set id [$cur_c create line 0 0 0 0 -fill black \
	-tags "origin icon icon_input"]
      $cur_c addtag inst$id withtag $id
      upvar #0 ${cur_s}_inst$id i_data
      set i_data(type) input
      $cur_c create line 0 0 -10 0 -15 -5 -25 -5 -25 5 -15 05 -10 0 \
    	-tags "icon inst$id" -fill black
      $cur_c create rectangle -1.67 -1.67 1.67 1.67 =
    	-tags "icon inst$id term {name input input $name} " -fill black -outline {}
      set i_data(_name) $name
      if {[catch {$cur_c create text -35 0 -text "$name" \
    	-font $FONT(standard,$fscale) \
    	-tags "icon inst$id size_standard scaletext" \
    	-anchor $XFORM($orient,0,e) \
    	-fill black -rotate 0} msg]} {lappend ICON_ERROR $msg}
      return $id
    }

Compiler makes certain assumptions for speed.
	Can't change structure of ICON
	Therefore, we needed generators



Data Structures
----------------

SUE's data structures are associative arrays whose array name is
(usually) a concatenation of keywords

All the canvases in SUE are in the "SUE" array

    sue> parray SUE
    SUE(ICON_test)      = ICON_test
    SUE(no_name)        = no_name
    SUE(test)           = test
    SUE(test_placement) = test_placement

Data about each canvas is in the "data" array
	upvar #0 SUE_$cur_s data

    sue> parray SUE_test
    SUE_test(auto_save)           = schedule
    SUE_test(canvas)              = .win1.c_test
    SUE_test(dir)                 = /proj/tech/foo/
    SUE_test(ever_modified)       = M
    SUE_test(filename)            = /proj/tech/foo/test.sue
    SUE_test(generator)           = 0
    SUE_test(icon_index)          = 3
    SUE_test(locked)              =
    SUE_test(modified)            = M
    SUE_test(modify_icon)         = M
    SUE_test(netlist_icon_index)  = 2
    SUE_test(netlist_level_index) = 0
    SUE_test(scale)               = 8
    SUE_test(suffix)              = .sue
    SUE_test(type)                = S
    SUE_test(written)             =

Generic data about each icon is in the "g_data" array
    upvar #0 icon_<cell> g_data

    sue> parray icon_INVA
    icon_INVA(_M)            = $M
    icon_INVA(_M,choices)    =
    icon_INVA(_M,default)    =
    icon_INVA(_dpc)          = $dpc
    icon_INVA(_dpc,choices)  =
    icon_INVA(_dpc,default)  =
    icon_INVA(_name)         = $name
    icon_INVA(_name,choices) =
    icon_INVA(_name,default) =
    icon_INVA(_verilog)      = INVA [unique_name "" $name INVA]
    (.in($in), .out($out))\;
    icon_INVA(arglist)       = $name $M $dpc
    icon_INVA(creator)       = INVA
    icon_INVA(defaults)      = {origin {0 0}} {orient R0} {name {}} {M {}} {dpc {}}
    icon_INVA(gargs)         =
    icon_INVA(gdefaults)     = {cell INVA {radio INVA INVB INVC INVD INVE INVA_ INVB_ INVC_ INVD_ INVE_}}
    icon_INVA(generator)     = INVA
    icon_INVA(prop_names)    = name M dpc


Data Structures (cont.)
-----------------------

Data about a specific instance of an icon is in the "i_data" array
      upvar #0 ${cur_s}_inst${id} i_data

    sue> parray test_inst138
    test_inst138(_M)     =
    test_inst138(_dpc)   =
    test_inst138(_name)  = [2:0]
    test_inst138(orient) = R0
    test_inst138(type)   = INVA

To change an attribute of a specific instance
	change in "i_data"
	remake id

	For example, to change the "dpc" property to "foo" of above

    sue> set test_inst138(_dpc) foo
    foo
    sue> remake 138 138
    145

	Note: this changes the ids of all the components of the
	icon instance, i.e. they are deleted and recreated.

Undo
	create and save the procedure to make the old version
	remember the new id to delete
	forward reference the ids with an associative array


Modes
------

SUE has modes which let you do multikey operations
	different hotkeys in modes
	different cursor (hand)

Modes:
	wire, line, arc, text, icon, duplication, replace, move,
	select region, zoom, pan

TK only allows one set of bindings per window.
	  "save_bindings", "clear_bindings" to enter a mode
	  "clear_bindings", "restore_bindings" to leave a mode

Add binding with bind_add, for example:

  bind_add -mode wire -hotkey Button-1 \
      -command "set SAVE(HV) 1; begin_draw_wire $SNAP_XY" \
      -help "Begin drawing a wire at the cursor position."

When entering a mode, give an abort command, for example:

  enter_mode wire abort_wire_mode

	If another command is started, or something goes
	wrong, the abort command is executed

When done, use leave_mode, for example:

  leave_mode wire




Debugging:
----------

When you get a SUE error you can typically get a TCL stack trace 
to help you debug it.  This stack trace is hidden from the user.
To see it, go to the SUE command line and type:

	set error

This is different from the "errorInfo" TCL variable that usually
gets overwritten as SUE is trying to catch the real error.


