Netlisting (cont.)
-------------------

Terminal priorities are used for determining names

    set TERM_PRIORITY(term) 0
    set TERM_PRIORITY(input) 5
    set TERM_PRIORITY(output) 5
    set TERM_PRIORITY(inout) 5
    set TERM_PRIORITY(name) 10
    set TERM_PRIORITY(global) 20


TERMS array is only a cache for wires (used to not include wires at
all).  If a wire is not in TERMS, traces wire until it finds something
that is in TERMS.  Thus you can move things (to a point) and change
wires and still cross-probe and select entire wires.

To speed up searchs, the RTERMS array (Reverse TERMS) is generated
from the TERMS array.  It associates names with ids.
	upvar #0 RTERMS_$cur_s RTERMS

    sue> parray RTERMS_no_name
    RTERMS_no_name(INVA)     = 88
    RTERMS_no_name(foo)      = 89 95 100
    RTERMS_no_name(uc_net_1) = 90

Note: only generated when needed.


Netlisting of a schematic consists of the following:
	determine hierarchy
	check for up-to-date cache
	generate terminal names
	walk thru each instance and netlist it

	Note: wires/IOs only used for generate terminal names


All netlisting information is saved to a netlist cache

    sue> exec more no_name.vg.cache
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name,cells) {{icon INVA has_schematic}}
    set netlist_props verilog
    set netlist_level 1000
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name,warnings) {{NETLIST WARNING: Unconnected net on terminal "out" in icon "INVA" (1) in schematic "no_name".}}
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name,version) MMI_SUE4.0
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name) {{module no_name (foo);} {   input           foo;} { } {     wire            uc_net_1;} { } {        INVA INVA (.in(foo), .out(uc_net_1));} {} {endmodule            // no_name} {}}
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name,names) {{450 290 {0 INVA}} {360 290 {1 foo}} {500 290 {0 uc_net_1}} {420 290 {0 foo}}}
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name,wires) {{360 290 420 290 foo}}
    set NETLIST_CACHE(no_name,dpc) {{{0 0 0 INVA INVA 10 2 {{foo 5.0 1.0} {uc_net_1 5.0 1.0}} {}}} {} {2 10 1} {}}


The NETLIST_CACHE(<cell>,names) line is effectively the TERMS array
except it is not referenced to ids (which can change) but to
coordinates on the screen.  For uniqueness, it specifies the nth thing on
the screen at those coordinates.


Making Changes to SUE
-----------------------

You can change many of SUE's source files and then reload them either by
	source ~/mysue/prop_edit.tcl
		OR
	set SOURCE_DIR ~/mysue
	reload prop_edit

Some things can't be changed and reloaded since they already affected 
stuff at startup

SUE has a patch mechanism
	can't simple source fixed procedures in .suerc files since
	.suerc is loaded before body of SUE

	any files in the directories:

		${MMI_TOOLS}/sue 
		${MMI_TOOLS}/../mmi_local/sue 
		${MMI_TOOLS}/patches
		~/mmi_private/sue
		$PATCH_DIR   (sue global variable)

	with the suffix 

		.sue_patch$VERSION_NUMBER

	will get loaded AFTER all other SUE procedures

	for example, you fix a bug in the "netlist" procedure and want
	to get the changes.  Put the new netlist procedure into a file in
	the appropriate place:

		~cad/mmi_local/sue/fix.sue_patch4.0


Use "mkcsue" to make a "compiled" version of SUE
	need to copy the entire SUE source tree (currently in ~tavrow/SUE4.0)
	to make a new version of SUE:

		cd build
		mkcsue

	you need to modify the "sue" startup script to then point to
	the correct executable to run this new version of SUE without
	installing it:

		#set cmdpath = ${MMI_TOOLS}/sue
		set cmdpath = /home/tavrow/sue4.0/build/release
